# mediaplayer — PRD

A minimal desktop audio player for MP3 and WAV files, written in pure C with SDL2 for windowing/audio and Nuklear for the GUI. One window, one file at a time, transport controls that just work.

## Goals

- Load and play a single `.wav` or `.mp3` file chosen by the user.
- Play / pause / stop, seek anywhere in the track, volume control.
- Accurate current-time / total-time display.
- Small, readable C99 codebase a person (or an AI session) can hold in their head.
- Builds cleanly on macOS with `clang` + `make` (Linux should work too).

## Non-goals

- Playlists, library management, or multiple tracks queued.
- Visualizations, theming, or skins.
- Formats beyond WAV and MP3 (no FLAC/OGG yet).
- Tag editing or metadata display beyond the filename.
- Windows support (happy accident if it happens, not a requirement).

## Tech stack

| Piece      | Choice                                     | Why                                              |
|------------|--------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| Language   | C99, single binary `mediaplayer`           | The point of the exercise                        |
| Window/GUI | SDL2 + Nuklear (SDL2 renderer backend)     | Official Nuklear demo backend, well-trodden path |
| WAV decode | `SDL_LoadWAV`                              | Built into SDL, zero extra deps                  |
| MP3 decode | [minimp3](https://github.com/lieff/minimp3) (single-header, public domain) | No SDL_mixer dependency, easy seeking control    |
| Build      | `make` (Makefile), optional CMake later     | Simplest thing that works                        |

Prerequisite: `brew install sdl2`. Nuklear and minimp3 are vendored into `vendor/` (headers, no build system of their own).

## Architecture

```
+------------------------------------------------------+
| main()                                               |
|  - SDL window + renderer                             |
|  - Nuklear context (sdl renderer backend)            |
|  - UI draw loop (60 fps-ish, vsync)                  |
+---------------------------+--------------------------+
                            | mutex-protected state
+---------------------------v--------------------------+
| playback engine (playback.c)                         |
|  - state: STOPPED / PLAYING / PAUSED                 |
|  - decoder: opens file, decodes PCM in a thread,     |
|    pushes into a lock-free-ish ring buffer           |
|  - SDL audio callback: pulls PCM from ring buffer,   |
|    applies volume, tracks samples-consumed           |
+------------------------------------------------------+
```

- **One audio device**, opened once at 44100 Hz / stereo / float32. All decoded audio is converted to that format (`SDL_AudioStream` for WAV resampling, minimp3 outputs whatever the file is and gets converted the same way).
- **Decoder thread** refills the ring buffer ahead of playback; the audio callback only ever reads from the ring buffer and applies volume.
- **Shared state** (position, state, filename, duration) behind one mutex; UI reads it every frame, engine writes it from callback/thread.
- **Track abstraction** (`track_open`, `track_decode_chunk`, `track_seek`, `track_close`) so WAV and MP3 are interchangeable behind one interface. WAV implementation = read whole file into memory via `SDL_LoadWAV`. MP3 implementation = minimp3 streaming decode.

## Functional requirements

1. Opening a file (drag-and-drop or Open button) stops current playback and starts the new track from 0:00.
2. Play/pause toggles without a gap or glitch; stop resets position to 0 and unload state to STOPPED.
3. Seeking: drag the progress slider; playback continues from the new position when released.
4. Time display shows `current / total` in `m:ss`.
5. Volume slider 0–100, applied at mix time in the audio callback, takes effect immediately.
6. When a track ends naturally, state returns to STOPPED with position 0.
7. Corrupt or unsupported files show an error label in the UI and leave playback state clean.
8. Keyboard: Space = play/pause, ←/→ = seek ∓/±5 s, ↑/↓ = volume, Esc = quit.
9. Window is resizable; the Nuklear layout adapts (progress bar stretches).

## Success criteria

- `make` produces `./mediaplayer` with zero warnings under `-Wall -Wextra`.
- A 5-minute WAV plays through with no dropouts; pause/resume is instant.
- A 5-minute MP3 plays through; seek to the middle lands within ~1 second.
- Dragging a `.mp3` from Finder onto the window starts playback.
- Quitting mid-playback exits within 1 second with no leaked audio device warnings.

